== why the one disagreement is the frozen behavior, not the harness ==
The frozen AnalyzeRule.kt:686 splits a rule on the literal "##":
    val ruleStrS = rule.split("##")
So "#meta@text##忘语##忘语先生##" has four fields (the fourth empty), and lines 694-695 sets
replaceFirst = true when ruleStrS.size > 3. Kotlin's split keeps the trailing empty field.
Same-host check with the frozen build's Kotlin 2.1.0 stdlib:
$ java -cp <kotlin-stdlib-2.1.0.jar>;. SplitProbe
size=4 parts=[#meta@text, 忘语, 忘语先生, ]
The adapter implements the Java-split reading (3 fields, replace-all), so it prints
"作者：忘语先生"; the handset prints "忘语先生". The row is a finding for the adapter and
stays out of the promotion; the corpus expectation was not edited.

